{
  "_comment": "Four parts, at least two players each. No transposition: each phrase entry lists the pitches that part may use, and those are the keys that light up on the piano. Names (Eb3, G#4, A2), MIDI numbers, or fractional numbers for anything off the 12-tone grid (57.5 is a quarter-tone above Bb2). An empty or missing notes list frees the pitch entirely. tractDelta is added to the phrase preset's tract length, so the lower parts get a bigger body as well as lower notes. || HOW PITCH IS DECIDED: each phrase carries \"pitch\", one of four words. \"choose\" - the player picks from notes (this is also what leaving the key out means). \"fixed\" - the one written pitch, put back if a player wanders off it. \"assign\" - the notes are shared out among the players on that part by seat, cycling, so with 2 notes and 3 players you get 1,2,1 and both are always heard; the conductor's desk warns when a part has fewer players than notes. \"drift\" - notes are the EDGES of a band, not a list: the lowest and the highest light up on the piano along with every key between them, and the player clicks anywhere inside to move the pitch. The page never changes the pitch by itself and J does nothing here - sliding is the hand, and how much of each move you hear as a slide is the pitch glide in timing and voice, which runs to 2s. Example for slip, slide, perish: { \"pitch\": \"drift\", \"notes\": [\"D2\", \"A2\"] }. || Instruction text lives in cues.json, not here.",
